## Idempotency Key Implementation

```java
// Idempotency record stored in Redis or DB:
@Entity
public class IdempotencyRecord {
    @Id
    private String key;  // Client-provided UUID
    private String requestHash;  // Hash of request body (detect same key, different body)
    private int responseStatus;
    private String responseBody;  // Serialized response
    private LocalDateTime createdAt;
    // TTL managed by scheduled cleanup or Redis TTL
}

// Interceptor or filter:
@Component
public class IdempotencyFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    @Autowired
    private IdempotencyRepository idempotencyRepo;

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request,
                                     HttpServletResponse response,
                                     FilterChain chain) throws IOException, ServletException {
        String idempotencyKey = request.getHeader("Idempotency-Key");

        if (idempotencyKey == null || !isModifyingMethod(request.getMethod())) {
            chain.doFilter(request, response);  // No key — pass through
            return;
        }

        // Check if this key was already processed:
        Optional<IdempotencyRecord> existing = idempotencyRepo.findById(idempotencyKey);
        if (existing.isPresent()) {
            IdempotencyRecord record = existing.get();
            // Return stored response:
            response.setStatus(record.getResponseStatus());
            response.setContentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE);
            response.getWriter().write(record.getResponseBody());
            response.setHeader("Idempotency-Replay", "true");  // Signal to client
            return;
        }

        // First time: process and store result:
        ContentCachingResponseWrapper responseWrapper =
            new ContentCachingResponseWrapper(response);
        chain.doFilter(request, responseWrapper);

        // Store the response:
        IdempotencyRecord record = new IdempotencyRecord();
        record.setKey(idempotencyKey);
        record.setResponseStatus(responseWrapper.getStatus());
        record.setResponseBody(new String(responseWrapper.getContentAsByteArray()));
        record.setCreatedAt(LocalDateTime.now());
        idempotencyRepo.save(record);

        responseWrapper.copyBodyToResponse();
    }

    private boolean isModifyingMethod(String method) {
        return "POST".equals(method) || "PATCH".equals(method);
    }
}

// Client usage:
HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
headers.set("Idempotency-Key", UUID.randomUUID().toString());  // Generate once, retry with same
RestTemplate restTemplate = new RestTemplate();
ResponseEntity<Order> response = restTemplate.exchange(
    "/api/orders", HttpMethod.POST, new HttpEntity<>(request, headers), Order.class
);
// If network fails: retry with SAME idempotency key — safe
```

## Production Reality

Idempotency keys are non-negotiable for payment endpoints, order creation, and any operation that can't be safely duplicated. Stripe makes it the client's responsibility to provide the key; the server's job is to honor it. Use Redis with TTL for idempotency storage — it handles expiry automatically and is fast enough for the per-request lookup overhead.

## Content Negotiation in Spring MVC

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/orders")
public class OrderController {

    // Accepts both JSON and XML request bodies:
    @PostMapping(
        consumes = {MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE, MediaType.APPLICATION_XML_VALUE},
        produces = MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE  // Always responds with JSON
    )
    public ResponseEntity<Order> createOrder(@RequestBody CreateOrderRequest req) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED).body(orderService.create(req));
    }

    // Different endpoints for different formats (alternative approach):
    @GetMapping(value = "/{id}", produces = MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE)
    public Order getOrderAsJson(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return orderService.findById(id);
    }

    @GetMapping(value = "/{id}", produces = MediaType.APPLICATION_XML_VALUE)
    public Order getOrderAsXml(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return orderService.findById(id);
    }
    // Spring picks the right mapping based on Accept header
}
```

## Custom HttpMessageConverter

```java
// For a custom format (CSV, Protocol Buffers, etc.):
@Component
public class CsvHttpMessageConverter extends AbstractHttpMessageConverter<List<?>> {

    public CsvHttpMessageConverter() {
        super(MediaType.valueOf("text/csv"));
    }

    @Override
    protected boolean supports(Class<?> clazz) {
        return List.class.isAssignableFrom(clazz);
    }

    @Override
    protected List<?> readInternal(Class<? extends List<?>> clazz,
                                    HttpInputMessage inputMessage) throws IOException {
        // Parse CSV from input stream
        return parseCsv(inputMessage.getBody());
    }

    @Override
    protected void writeInternal(List<?> objects, HttpOutputMessage outputMessage) throws IOException {
        // Write objects as CSV
        PrintWriter writer = new PrintWriter(outputMessage.getBody());
        objects.forEach(obj -> writer.println(toCsvLine(obj)));
        writer.flush();
    }
}

// Endpoint that returns CSV when Accept: text/csv:
@GetMapping(value = "/export", produces = "text/csv")
public List<Order> exportOrders() {
    return orderService.findAll();
    // Jackson writes JSON normally; CsvHttpMessageConverter writes CSV when Accept: text/csv
}
```

## Production Reality

For most REST APIs, JSON is sufficient and you don't need to configure content negotiation explicitly — Spring Boot auto-configures Jackson for JSON. The cases where you need it: supporting XML for legacy clients, providing CSV exports, or integrating with systems that use Protocol Buffers. Always set `produces = MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE` explicitly on endpoints where it matters — it makes the contract clear and returns 406 Not Acceptable instead of silently returning the wrong format.
